p\":1782967159241,\"selection\":[{\"actionHash\":\"uhCkkrRi-hTIIKO4rIu-_W84SjrD6GWgPqHjSQyyYHp5-ANQf5AiG\",\"id\":\"486471\",\"content\":\"Agents attach their workproduct to nodes as output artifacts\"}]},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\",\"timestamp\":1782967159241},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"Confirmed — the reframed node committed. Now let's build task 2: group artifacts into **Inputs** vs **Workproduct/Outputs** sections rather than leaning on the `type` field alone. Let me read the relevant code first.Now the SCSS and a sample test for conventions.Now I'll build it. First the pure classifier module:Now refactor `ArtifactsField` to render grouped. First the import:Now extract the row renderer and render grouped sections:Now the SCSS for the group label:Now the unit tests:Now typecheck the touched files:Let me run the full suite to confirm nothing regressed:All green — 283/283, touched files typecheck clean. Now posting the result back onto the node as a draft:Built, verified, and the result draft is open on `#486471`.\\n\\n## What I built\\nGrouping into **sections** (your call — derived from `type`, not a new `role` field):\\n\\n- **`web/src/artifactGroups.ts`** (pure) — `artifactGroup(a)` classifies by type (`workproduct`/`output` → **Workproduct**; everything else, including `conversation`/`conversation-ref` since those are retained clarity inputs → **Clarity inputs**), and `groupArtifacts(items)` partitions the list **preserving each artifact's original index**, so the editor's edit/remove callbacks still hit the real list position.\\n- **`ArtifactsField.tsx`** — extracted `renderRow()` and now renders two labelled sections. Headers show **only when both groups are present**, so a node carrying only inputs stays clean (no lone \\\"Inputs\\\" header).\\n- **SCSS** — a small uppercase `.artifact-group-label`.\\n\\n## Verification\\n- `artifactGroups.test.ts` — **6/6** (classify by type incl. case/trim, conversation→input, empty→input; partition preserves indices + order; all-input / all-output / empty).\\n- Full suite **283/283**; `tsc` clean on touched files.\\n\\n## In the draft\\nTask 2 checked; spec records the decision + implementation (grouping derived from type, headers only on a mix); the executable criterion stays `met`; the **human** criterion carries build evidence but is left **unconfirmed** — so the leaf reads as **awaiting your evaluation** rather than self-marked Achieved. Nothing commits until you Confirm.\\n\\nOne decision worth your eye on review: I put the output set at `{workproduct, output}` and treated **conversations as inputs**. If you'd rather conversations be their own third section (or workproduct include other types), say so and I'll adjust the one `OUTPUT_ARTIFACT_TYPES` set.\\n\\nTo see it live after Confirm: open a node that has both a `design`/`doc` and a `workproduct` artifact (e.g. this very node once committed) — you'll get the two headers.\",\"timestamp\":1782967159241,\"toolCalls\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01Wh1oXPKf6vaAZg5Yhwtn2C\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01Vkw5HDMndtKbf3GVfwB9ov\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01C7RGuLjnYr151YmmK5buRE\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01Lq6uS2aGPkqX5rsnj84HFA\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_013EeUhXCAXdTD2yEQZugfrE\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01FSwFa8HJuCmb5Zf9tsPNZo\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01CwGben9yDERkmjNJCL2sYR\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01Wkg5Ah5xrLreayRsUsowiT\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01CLfYUABfB6L6XxubHoY6WJ\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"failed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01VrVxD1HA8A2e2CVyd92jdg\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01RQ8TaVfDyvjY4PEGUJ5SPr\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01W3Vad4Wxp8fMLxGY76crot\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_015Ya7kZn9uH1uzirEU6PpbJ\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01NbTDfS9Kd6aY1JYFquThhY\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01FTbdw9dGA2bynPx5fGpAwo\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_018YFCKvvtLcTfaput2f75LC\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01KQ4sh3ghjKEoUzTvWu6uWR\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"it looks like you just added tags.  What I thought this would do is add a subsection section in the artifacts section for added workproducts.  I'm not sure a tag is useful\",\"timestamp\":1782967624624},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\",\"timestamp\":1782967624624},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"You're right to flag that — let me clear up two things and then make it a real subsection.\\n\\n**On \\\"tags\\\":** I didn't touch this node's tags — the `Artifact` / `Completion` tags on it are pre-existing category tags, not something I added. And separately: the draft that was supposed to *record* the grouping work on this node (check task 2, note the decision) looks like it was **discarded** — the live node still shows task 2 unchecked. The grouping *code* did land on disk, though. What you saw as \\\"tags\\\" is almost certainly my group **labels** — I rendered them as small uppercase text, which reads like a tag/badge rather than a real subsection, and they only appeared when a node had *both* inputs and outputs (so on a workproduct-only node you'd see nothing).\\n\\nYour intent is clear and I agree: a genuine **Workproduct subsection** inside the artifacts area. Let me strengthen it to that. First, current state of the render:Now make it a real bounded subsection — the Workproduct group shows whenever there are outputs, each group wrapped in its own titled block:Now the SCSS — turn the label into a real bounded subsection (the Workproduct group gets a tinted, left-accented block so it reads as its own area):Green — 6/6, tsc clean. Now re-proposing the node update (the earlier one was discarded), corrected to say **subsection**, not tag/badge:Fixed. To be clear on what changed vs. what you were seeing:\\n\\n- **No tags were added by me.** The `Artifact`/`Completion` chips on this node are its pre-existing category tags. What looked tag-like was my earlier *group label* — small uppercase text that also only appeared when a node had both inputs and outputs, so on a workproduct-only node you'd have seen nothing.\\n- **Now it's a real subsection.** Each group renders as its own titled block, and the **Workproduct** block is a tinted, left-accented bounded area (same visual family as the conversation card) that groups the agent-produced outputs together. It shows **whenever the node has any workproduct artifacts** — even if there are no inputs. The \\\"Clarity inputs\\\" heading only appears when there are also inputs to distinguish from, so input-only nodes stay clean.\\n\\nGrouping is still derived from the artifact `type` (`workproduct`/`output` → Workproduct; everything else → inputs) — no new field.\\n\\nVerified: `artifactGroups` tests 6/6, `tsc` clean on touched files.\\n\\nThe tree draft is open again (the previous one recording this was discarded — that's why task 2 still showed unchecked). It marks task 2 done with the corrected \\\"subsection, not a tag/badge\\\" wording and the human criterion left for you to confirm visually. Once you Confirm and reload, open this node: its `workproduct` files should sit inside a distinct **Workproduct** block, separate from any input references.\",\"timestamp\":1782967624624,\"toolCalls\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_019rGjuThywT2M4QqssVwp26\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01Kkg7UiZX1nhajeWYQTPPNQ\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01JMEAbHwgakSK6j3ZQoTXHx\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01GV1uPHs7GMtdRiyTQsb3kx\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01SxPuRKJBs6iyHdVQ4unKwH\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"there's no need to render the \\\"workproduct\\\"  type anymore then for each one, or, that should be more specific like: modified_code or PR or something else, not just duplicated \\\"workproduct.   I  also note that when you propose a change my two options are confirm/discard.   I think it would be helpful to have \\\"refine\\\" which delcares that I like part of what I see but need to work on some part.\",\"timestamp\":1782967905801},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\",\"timestamp\":1782967905801},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"Two good points — both are design calls, so let me capture them in the tree (as a draft) rather than just code them, and flag the real decisions in each.\\n\\n**1. Redundant \\\"workproduct\\\" per row.** You're right: once outputs live in a labelled *Workproduct* subsection, repeating `workproduct` on every row is noise. But it exposes a fork in what I built — grouping keys off `type ∈ {workproduct, output}`, so if rows instead carry *specific* kinds (PR, modified-code, log) they'd no longer group. So it's one of: **(a)** enumerate an output-*kind* set (pr / modified-code / file / build-log / …) that all group as Workproduct and render the specific kind per row; or **(b)** add a `role` field so `type` is free-form-specific and grouping reads `role`. You leaned earlier toward derive-from-type (no role field) → **(a)** fits that. Either way, stop showing the generic label inside the section.\\n\\n**2. A third \\\"Refine\\\" option.** Agreed — Confirm/Discard is all-or-nothing; \\\"Refine\\\" (keep what works, hand the rest back to the agent to iterate) is a real gap in the draft-review flow. That's its own leaf under the draft-review branch.\\n\\nProposing both as a draft:Draft's open — one update, one new node.\\n\\n**On `#486471` (workproduct grouping):** task 2 (the subsection) is marked done-in-code; I added two new tasks capturing your point — the redundant-label fix, gated on the **(a) enumerate output kinds vs (b) add a role field** decision. The human criterion now explicitly asks for a *specific kind per row, not a repeated \\\"workproduct.\\\"* I didn't pick the fork for you — it's the same derive-from-type vs role-field choice from before, and you leaned toward derive-from-type (which is option **a**). Confirm the direction and I'll build it: expand the classifier to an output-kind set and drop the generic label inside the subsection.\\n\\n**New leaf under the draft-review branch:** *\\\"A human can Refine a draft — keep what works and hand the rest back to the agent.\\\"* It sits alongside the Confirm/Discard leaf and relies on the per-node accept/reject that's already built (that shapes *which* changes; Refine continues the *conversation* on the unsettled ones). Its one open decision is baked in as a task: **commit the accepted subset now and reopen a draft for the rest, vs keep everything pending until a final Confirm.**\\n\\nTwo things for your review:\\n1. Which fork for the output-kind question (a or b)? Once you say, the label cleanup is a small build.\\n2. For Refine, which of the two commit-semantics do you want — or leave it as an open decision on the leaf for now?\\n\\nNothing's committed until you Confirm (or Discard, or — soon — Refine 🙂).\",\"timestamp\":1782967905801,\"toolCalls\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01BSkFhBwxELhPB4jncmd72A\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"I think we use the \\\"label\\\" section for the type.  Right now the label is duplicating the file name, which makes no sense.  In fact I see other stuff in there that I don't quite understand it's being uses more like a description.  but in any case that's where that role can go and it can just be free form for now.\",\"timestamp\":1782968227975},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\",\"timestamp\":1782968227975},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"Eric confirmed the last draft — the **Refine** leaf is now live under the draft-review branch, and `#486471` carries the two new tasks. Now your decision on the output-kind fork:\\n\\nYou've resolved it — neither enumerate a type-set nor add a `role` field: **the free-form kind/role lives in the existing `label`**. And you've flagged a real muddle: today `label` often just repeats the filename (which is already the `uri`) or drifts into a description. Let me record that decision on the node and lay out exactly what I'd render, so I don't guess wrong on the UI:Draft's open — decision recorded on `#486471`: kind/role lives free-form in `label`, `type` stays the grouping key, classifier unchanged.\\n\\nHere's the concrete rendering I'd build, so you can correct in one line before I touch code. Today each artifact row shows three editable inputs — `type` | `label` | `uri`. The change:\\n\\n- **`label`** becomes the row's primary **kind** field (placeholder like *\\\"kind — PR / modified-code / doc\\\"*), no longer a filename echo.\\n- **`uri`** stays as the path/link.\\n- **`type`** is no longer shown as a per-row input inside a group — the section header (Workproduct / Clarity inputs) conveys it. `type` still lives in the data as the grouping key (agents keep setting `workproduct`); a human adding a fresh artifact gets one that defaults into the inputs group unless they mark it otherwise.\\n\\nThe one thing I want to confirm: **should the `type` field disappear from the row entirely** (label = the sole descriptor, as above), or stay as a small editable so a human can still flip an artifact between input/output? I lean \\\"disappear, label-only\\\" per your \\\"free form for now\\\" — but it's your call, and it's the difference between a clean row and keeping a manual input/output toggle.\\n\\nSay which and I'll build it (adjust `ArtifactsField` rendering + a test), then post results back on the node. Nothing's committed until