      "evidence": "2026-06-30 BUILT, awaiting Eric live verify: a conversation artifact with a real transcript renders as a readable thread (role badge + content, ask-time anchor, collapsible plan); an empty/placeholder one (like the seed on branch-transcript, uri='') now shows an explicit 'no transcript captured yet' card rather than a blank editable row. conversationArtifact.test.ts 5/5 (pure layer); tsc clean. To see a populated thread, use the Attach-conversation control (sibling) to fill a node's conversation artifact, then open that node.",
      "met": true,
      "by": "uhCAk6NMsJYZovJRS4CRK3hQsv5bN5pWENojRKPEA3x8grOccqulR",
      "at": 1782962694594
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "workproduct",
      "label": "conversationArtifact.ts — pure: CONVERSATION_ARTIFACT_TYPE + isConversationArtifact / makeConversationArtifact / conversationTranscript",
      "uri": "web/src/harness/conversationArtifact.ts"
    },
    {
      "type": "workproduct",
      "label": "ConversationArtifact.tsx — read-only thread widget + empty-state card (transcript=null)",
      "uri": "web/src/components/OutcomeFieldsEditor/ConversationArtifact.tsx"
    },
    {
      "type": "workproduct",
      "label": "ArtifactsField.tsx — detects a conversation artifact BY TYPE and renders the thread / empty state",
      "uri": "web/src/components/OutcomeFieldsEditor/ArtifactsField.tsx"
    },
    {
      "type": "workproduct",
      "label": "OutcomeFieldsEditor.scss — .conversation-* styles incl. empty-state",
      "uri": "web/src/components/OutcomeFieldsEditor/OutcomeFieldsEditor.scss"
    },
    {
      "type": "workproduct",
      "label": "conversationArtifact.test.ts — 5 tests: make/detect/round-trip/non-conversation/malformed/null-safe",
      "uri": "web/test/conversationArtifact.test.ts"
    },
    {
      "type": "conversation",
      "label": "lets flesh out this node. propose any leafs that need to …",
      "uri": "{\"version\":1,\"sessionId\":\"f34ab46f-3496-46eb-afa0-40e44f8ab62e\",\"title\":\"lets flesh out this node. propose any leafs that need to …\",\"capturedAt\":1782963028436,\"turns\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"lets flesh out this node. propose any leafs that need to be added to make this work.\"},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"I'll ground this in the live tree first, then propose a fleshed-out node plus the child leaves it needs. Let me load the Acorn tool schemas.The live tree is too large to pull inline, but I have the current snapshot in context and the key hashes I need. I'll work from that.\\n\\nHere's my read of **`branch-transcript`** (\\\"The conversation that shaped a branch is retained as an artifact on that branch\\\"): today it's a single `Small` leaf, but making it real needs three distinct things that don't fit one buildable leaf — (1) *capturing* the live chat as data, (2) a *conversation artifact type* that renders a transcript readably, and (3) a *control* to attach a session to a node. That's a branch, not a leaf. I'll convert it to an `Uncertain` branch, sharpen its outcome/spec into a self-contained target-state, give it a branch-level integration criterion, and propose the three child leaves.\\n\\nProposing this as a draft now:Draft's open for your review (nothing committed yet). Here's what I proposed:\\n\\n**`branch-transcript` → converted from a leaf to an `Uncertain` branch.** Its outcome/spec now read as a self-contained target-state, with a single branch-level **integration criterion** (human): the chat that shaped a node can be attached *and* shows up on that node as a readable thread, end to end. The old leaf taskList is dropped — the children carry it now.\\n\\n**Three child leaves, ordered left→right by what's foundational:**\\n\\n1. **Capture the session as a serializable transcript** *(most foundational)* — record the HarnessChat stream (turns, tool calls, proposed edits) into a JSON-serializable transcript. Executable criterion: serialize/parse round-trips. *Relies-on the ACP chat-panel node that owns the stream.*\\n\\n2. **A `conversation` artifact type that renders as a readable thread** — extends branch E's typed artifacts so a held transcript renders inline (not a bare link). *Relies-on branch E artifacts + the transcript shape.*\\n\\n3. **A control that attaches the active session to its branch** — an \\\"attach this conversation\\\" action in the chat panel that submits the transcript as a `propose_edits` draft adding a conversation artifact to the chosen node. *Relies-on propose_edits + leaves 1 and 2.*\\n\\nThis keeps the dependencies real: each child is something the parent *needs*, and the cross-branch reliances (chat panel, branch E, propose_edits) are named in the specs rather than re-owned.\\n\\nTwo things worth a look when you review:\\n- The relies-on links to the chat-panel node, branch E, and propose_edits are written as prose in the specs for now (typed relies-on edges aren't surfaced yet). If you'd rather I model any as explicit connections, say so.\\n- Leaf 1's transcript shape and leaf 2's artifact type are the same conversation contract from two ends — fine as-is, but if you'd prefer them merged into one leaf, I can collapse them.\"},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"build this leaf\"},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\"},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"You committed the branch — the three children are live now. You've selected the **capture-transcript** leaf (`#185232`) and asked me to build it. This is the \\\"do the work\\\" path: implement the code, run the executable criterion, and post the verdict + workproduct back onto the node.\\n\\nLet me explore the relevant code first.Interesting — there's already a `chatHistory.ts` with uncommitted changes. Let me read the harness types, the existing chatHistory, and how HarnessChat manages its stream.The chat-session model already round-trips through JSON, but it's localStorage-shaped (keyed by session/agent) and `ChatMessage` lacks per-turn timestamps and tool-call/proposed-edit references. Let me check the harness types and the HarnessChat stream to see what's actually available to capture.Now let me see how HarnessChat accumulates the stream — particularly tool calls and proposed edits — so I capture from the right place.Tool calls are currently folded into message text, not retained structurally. Let me look at message creation and the proposed-edits seam to decide how much to wire for genuine capture.Let me check how the Acorn tool titles are recognized, so the transcript can flag propose_edits tool calls.I have the full picture. The chat-session model already round-trips through JSON but is localStorage-shaped and `ChatMessage` lacks per-turn timestamps and structured tool calls. My build:\\n\\n1. **`transcript.ts`** (pure) — a portable `Transcript` shape with ordered turns `{role, content, timestamp?, thinking?, toolCalls?}`, `buildTranscript` from a session, `serialize`/`parse` round-trip, and a derived `proposedEdits` view (propose_edits tool-call refs).\\n2. Extend `ChatMessage` with optional `at` + `toolCalls` so capture is *real*, and wire HarnessChat to stamp them.\\n3. Unit tests for the executable criterion.\\n\\nLet me start by extending the chat model.Now the pure transcript module:Now wire HarnessChat to stamp timestamps and record tool calls structurally. First the import:Now the unit tests for the executable criterion. Let me check the test setup conventions:All 9 pass. Now verify I didn't break the existing chatHistory tests or TypeScript on the touched files:Let me confirm the typecheck cleanly — the grep produced no output, which is ambiguous:Typecheck clean, both suites green. Now I'll follow the working loop's final step — post the verdict + workproduct artifacts back onto the leaf as a draft for you to confirm.Built and verified. The draft posting the result back onto the leaf is open for your review.\\n\\n## What I built\\n\\n**`web/src/harness/transcript.ts`** — a pure capture module:\\n- `Transcript { version, sessionId, agentName?, title, capturedAt, turns[] }`, where each `TranscriptTurn` is `{role, content, timestamp?, thinking?, toolCalls?}`.\\n- `buildTranscript(session, capturedAt)` projects a stored chat session into it; `serializeTranscript` / `parseTranscript` round-trip losslessly (parse normalizes drifted/legacy turns and rejects non-transcript input).\\n- `proposedEdits(t)` derives the propose_edits tool-call references — the \\\"proposed edits within them\\\" the spec asks the capture to preserve.\\n\\n**Made capture real at the source** (so it's not a placeholder shape):\\n- `chatHistory.ts` — `ChatMessage` gains optional `at` (per-turn timestamp) and structured `toolCalls`.\\n- `HarnessChat.tsx` — stamps `at` on each message and records `tool_call` updates structurally (newest-status-wins per id), so **message + thought + tool_call** are all retained distinct from the prose.\\n\\n## Verification (the executable criterion)\\n- `web/test/transcript.test.ts` — **9/9 pass**. Round-trip preserves turn order, roles, and content (incl. empty session); normalizes a drifted turn; rejects non-transcript JSON; proposed-edit refs extracted with turn index and survive the round-trip.\\n- Existing `chatHistory.test.ts` still **18/18**; `tsc --noEmit` clean on all touched files.\\n\\n## One honest caveat (in the node's spec + evidence)\\nThe agent **`plan`** stream is transient, session-level UI state not attached to any turn, so I deliberately left it out of the per-turn transcript — message/thought/tool_call are what's captured. I noted this on the node rather than silently claiming the full \\\"message/thought/plan/tool_call\\\" wording.\\n\\nThe draft marks the three tasks complete, sets the executable criterion `met`, attaches the four files as workproduct artifacts, and proposes `Achieved`. Nothing is committed until you Confirm. Next sibling when you're ready: the **conversation artifact type** that renders this transcript as a readable thread.\"},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"hmmm.  There's a bit of an issue here.  we may want the plan as it caries valuable information.  Also, there's a question of exactly which node transcripts should be attached to, which depends on context.  do you have a suggestion\",\"timestamp\":1782847992484},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\",\"timestamp\":1782847992484},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"Good questions — both are real, and they're connected. My take:\\n\\n## 1. The plan stream — yes, capture it\\n\\nYou're right that the plan carries intent the messages don't. The reason I left it out is structural, not value: the ACP `plan` update is *replace-wholesale* and **not attached to any turn** — it doesn't fit the `{role, content}` per-turn model. So the fix isn't to jam it into a turn, it's to capture it **at the session level as timestamped snapshots**:\\n\\n```\\nTranscript.planSnapshots?: [{ at, entries: [{content, priority, status}] }]\\n```\\n\\nEach `plan` update appends a snapshot. That preserves the *evolution* of the agent's intent (not just the final list), keeps the per-turn thread clean, and round-trips the same way. The conversation widget can render the latest plan as a header and let you expand the history. This reopens the capture leaf slightly (one more task), which is honest — it's genuine added scope.\\n\\n## 2. Which node to attach to — make it computable, not guessed\\n\\nThis is the better question. A session almost never shapes *one* node — this very session created a branch + 3 children, then built one leaf. So \\\"attach to the selected node\\\" is a weak default and \\\"attach to all touched nodes\\\" is noise (N copies of one artifact).\\n\\nMy suggestion: **default the target to the lowest common ancestor of the nodes the session actually edited**, with the human free to override.\\n\\nThe nice part: the capture leaf *already* preserves `proposedEdits` (turn index + target node ids). From those ids → the set of touched nodes → their LCA in the live tree. For this session that LCA is `branch-transcript` itself — exactly \\\"the branch it shaped.\\\" So:\\n- **one canonical transcript artifact**, attached to the branch the conversation was about (computed, not guessed);\\n- current selection as the fallback/override when there were no edits (a pure discussion);\\n- other nodes can later *reference* it (via `[[handle]]`) rather than duplicate it.\\n\\nThis also gives the three leaves a clean data flow: **capture** preserves proposed-edit refs → **attach** uses them to compute the LCA target → **conversation artifact** renders the one copy.\\n\\n---\\n\\nI'll propose a draft that encodes both decisions — reopening the capture leaf for plan-snapshots, and sharpening the attach leaf's target policy:Draft's open. It encodes both decisions:\\n\\n**Capture leaf** (`#185232`) — reopened to `NotAchieved` with a new task + a second executable criterion for plan-snapshots. The already-passing round-trip criterion keeps its `met: true` + evidence; only the plan work is outstanding. Spec now records the reversal (\\\"plan carries valuable intent worth keeping\\\") and specifies `planSnapshots = [{at, entries}]` captured per `plan` update, so the *evolution* of intent is kept, not just the final list.\\n\\n**Attach leaf** — sharpened so the target is **computed, not guessed**: LCA of the nodes the session edited (from the transcript's `proposedEdits` refs → tree), current selection as the no-edits fallback, human override, and **one canonical artifact** with `[[handle]]` references instead of duplicates.\\n\\nThat also wires the three siblings into a clean flow: capture preserves edit refs → attach derives the target from them → conversation artifact renders the single copy.\\n\\nTwo things to weigh on review:\\n- **Plan placement** — I put `planSnapshots` at the session level (separate from turns). The alternative is a `plan`-typed entry interleaved in the turn timeline, which reads more like a single chronological thread but muddies the per-turn `{role, content}` model. I lean session-level; say the word if you'd rather interleave.\\n- **LCA edge case** — if a session edits two unrelated branches, the LCA can float up to the root. Worth a guard: if the LCA is the root (or above a depth threshold), fall back to asking rather than dumping the transcript at the top. I can add that as a task if you want it handled now rather than later.\\n\\nWant me to build the plan-snapshot capture next, or adjust either decision first?\",\"timestamp\":1782847992484,\"toolCalls\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01HzjADMzfWrmsRZVKCVk8d6\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"one thing that needs fixing.  you say \\\"current selected node\\\"  I've notice that when you are thinking about things, I often go and look at other nodes which changes the \\\"current selected node\\\"   I think we have to have a notion of the node that was selected when a question was asked for that to work correctly\",\"timestamp\":1782849471857},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"That's a genuine correctness bug, not a nicety — the selection is live state and you move it while I work, so \\\"current selection\\\" at attach-time is the wrong anchor. The fix is to **capture the selection at ask-time and pin it**, never read it live. Let me confirm how the chat panel handles selection today so I ground the spec correctly.Confirmed, and it's a clean seam. Look at HarnessChat lines 595–597: at **send-time** the panel already reads the selection and prepends it to the turn — but only as prose in the prompt, thrown away after. So the selection-when-the-question-was-asked is *available*; it's just not retained. The fix is to persist it structurally onto the turn, and make the attach fallback read *that*, never live `readSelection`.\\n\\nSo:\\n- **Capture** records the send-time selection on each user turn → the conversation's anchor survives your later navigation.\\n- **Attach** fallback = the session's *anchored* selection (the node selected when the question was asked), never the current one.\\n\\nUpdating both leaves in the draft:Updated draft's open. The fix is captured as a real concept, not just a wording tweak:\\n\\n- **Capture leaf** gains a task + executable criterion: each user turn records the node(s) selected **at send-time** (`turn.selection`), persisting the selection HarnessChat already reads at ~line 595 instead of discarding it as prose. The criterion explicitly tests that the ask-time selection is **unaffected by selection changing afterward** — i.e. it pins.\\n\\n- **Attach leaf** fallback is now \\\"the session's **anchored** selection (selected when the question was asked),\\\" with a spec note flagging *\\\"never read the live current selection — it is mutable state the human moves while the agent works, so attaching to it is a known correctness bug.\\\"* I also added the root-LCA guard you'd want anyway.\\n\\nSo the anchor is a property of the *conversation*, fixed at ask-time and carried in the transcript — which means it survives across turns, reloads, and your wandering. Nothing committed until you Confirm.\\n\\nOne thing worth deciding when you build it: a session has *many* user turns, each with its own ask-time selection. For the no-edits fallback, which anchor wins — the **first** turn's selection (what framed the whole conversation) or the **most recent** ask's? I'd default to the first, but it's a judgment call I'd rather you make than guess.\",\"timestamp\":1782849471857,\"toolCalls\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_018PmsefzbEyW7DHsS7kp41n\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"id\":\"toolu_01XiNqAwkMdWCk6sEzPgg5cH\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"use the first\",\"timestamp\":1782849856946},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"Locking that in — the attach leaf's anchor is the **first** user turn's selection (what framed the conversation), not the latest.Done. The attach leaf now pins the no-edits fallback to the **first user turn's** ask-time selection — the spec, the task, and the human criterion all say \\\"first, not latest, not live,\\\" and the decision is stamped with the date and your call. The criterion even names the test: select different nodes on later turns and confirm the target stays the first one.\\n\\nDraft's open for review. The capture leaf still needs the actual build (plan-snapshots + per-turn send-time selection); say the word when you want me to implement it.\",\"timestamp\":1782849856946,\"toolCalls\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_019WHtKgC3wNhBY3A9eZafxD\",\"title\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"do it\",\"timestamp\":1782850626715},{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":\"↻ sent updated tree\",\"timestamp\":1782850626715},{\"role\":\"agent\",\"content\":\"You committed the attach-leaf decision (first-turn anchor is in), but the capture leaf reverted to its earlier Achieved version — so the plan-snapshots + per-turn-selection work it now depends on isn't built yet. \\\"Do it\\\" = build that. Let me check the selection shape first.`SelectedNode` already carries `actionHash` — perfect for a stable anchor. Let me see the persist effect and plan state wiring in HarnessChat.Everything I need. Building both pieces now. First, extend the chat model with `selection` (per-message) and `planSnapshots` (per-session):Now add `planSnapshots` to `SessionRecord` and thread it through `upsertSession` / `persistTurn`, plus a getter:Now extend the transcript module to carry per-turn `selection`, session-level `planSnapshots`, and an `anchorSelection` helper (the first user turn's ask-time selection):Now the normalization, `messageToTurn`, `buildTranscript`, `parseTranscript`, and the `anchorSelection` helper:Now wire HarnessChat. Add the imports:Add the snapshots ref and extend `appendMessage` to capture selection:\",\"timestamp\":178