  "outcome": "The draft-review controls offer a third choice beyond Confirm and Discard: Refine. When a human likes part of a proposed draft but wants to rework another part, Refine keeps the loop going — it hands the draft back to the agent with the human's per-change decisions (accepted / rejected / edited) plus a note on what still needs work — instead of forcing an all-or-nothing commit-or-drop. The conversation continues against the same draft rather than starting over.",
  "spec": "Add a 'Refine' action beside Confirm/Discard in the draft-review UI (branch L / draft pipeline). Refine neither commits nor discards: it keeps the draft alive and sends a structured hand-back to the harness — the accepted/rejected/edited state (from the per-node accept/reject-in-draft leaf) plus the human's note — so the agent revises the still-open parts and re-proposes into the same draft. Relies-on the per-node accept/reject-in-draft capability (the accepted-subset state) and the in-app chat loop (the hand-back channel), both already built.\n\nOPEN DECISION: does Refine commit the accepted subset immediately and reopen a fresh draft for the rest, or keep everything pending until a final Confirm? Resolve before building.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "The draft-review controls show Confirm / Refine / Discard; clicking Refine keeps the draft open and hands the human's accepted/edited/rejected state + note back to the agent, which revises the open parts — no commit, no discard",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkkZclfN1VdCbfyv3MCxLKeVYv1_NVVfBsET1.description:
{
  "outcome": "Working with agents is no longer serialized behind a single conversation. Several agent sessions run at once and a human moves freely between them while turns are in flight; the live tree stays editable the whole time, and nothing — a human edit, an agent turn, a pending draft — is silently lost, blocked, or stomped. Two pains this dissolves: fear of touching the tree while an agent works, and being locked to one conversation at a time.",
  "handle": "concurrent-sessions",
  "spec": "The transport is NOT the bottleneck — the sidecar already multiplexes sessions by sessionId and chat history is already per-session. Every serialization point is renderer-side: HarnessChat's single sessionRef/busy/transcript, the single global draft slice (state.ui.draft), the single global askConfirm modal, and propose_edits carrying no baseline so conflicts with concurrent human edits are undetectable by construction. A flow view of the concurrent loop (sessions × registry × per-session drafts × attention inbox) belongs here; the detailed specs live on the leaves. Build order left→right: session registry first (pure renderer unlock), then baseline/rebase (removes the fear, and its machinery is a dependency of per-session drafts), then the attention inbox, per-session drafts, scope/presence, cross-window hardening.",
  "principle": "A human's live edit is never silently overwritten by an agent's proposal, and a running turn is never cancelled or hidden by navigation.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "With two sessions running concurrently, a human edits the tree mid-turn, reviews each session's draft independently, and confirms both in either order — nothing lost, nothing blocked, no silent overwrite",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "doc",
      "label": "plan: concurrent agent sessions — bottleneck analysis + phased build order",
      "uri": "acorn-concurrent-sessions-plan.md"
    }
  ]
}

