uhCkkS5n6kQRwmPTeHOBTmh5DA_G5eUyhROaFxmK.description:
{
  "outcome": "A human edits the tree freely while agents work. Every agent proposal knows the baseline it was computed against, is rebased against the live tree at draft-open and at Confirm, and any change where both the human and the agent touched the same field surfaces as an explicit conflict — with the human's live edit winning by default. Silent overwrite becomes impossible in either direction, removing the reason to freeze while an agent runs.",
  "handle": "baseline-rebase",
  "spec": "Today the agent's snapshot is stamped nowhere and propose_edits diffs apply against whatever the tree is at Confirm time — a human edit made after the agent read is silently lost. This is the live-loop analogue of branch I's 'tolerant import' three-way merge leaf, built where it now belongs: on the draft pipeline. Turn-start snapshot-resend freshness already exists; this adds the baseline stamp, the per-node three-way rebase (live vs baseline on fields the diff touches), and conflict presentation in the review panel. Its rebase machinery is a declared dependency of [[per-session-drafts]].\n\nDECIDED 2026-07-28 (Eric): baseline identity is a SNAPSHOT HASH computed by a canonical stable-stringify (recursively sorted keys) + hash, NOT a monotonic counter. Rationale: content-derived identity cannot miss a mutation path (a counter must be bumped on every local mutation AND every incoming Holochain signal — one missed path silently breaks the very detection this leaf exists for); hashes computed in different windows over converged state agree by construction (needed by [[cross-window]]); no false drift on edit-then-revert; the per-turn cost (hashing ~140KB) is negligible. The renderer retains the baseline SNAPSHOT (natural home: the session registry's per-session lastTreeSent from [[session-registry]]) keyed by its hash — the hash is the drift-check comparator, the propose_edits wire token, the display/audit name, and the dedup key; the retained content drives the field-level three-way merge. Integrity: the agent passes back only the token and the renderer looks up the snapshot it actually served, so an agent cannot fabricate a baseline that makes an overwrite look conflict-free. Determinism is fail-safe: a false hash MISMATCH merely triggers a rebase that finds zero field diffs (wasted milliseconds); a false MATCH cannot occur short of hash collision since the hash covers the actual serialized bytes. Note the nested-JSON trap does not apply: a node's fields live in `description` as a string and are hashed as opaque bytes — identical to how computeProjectDiff already compares nodes.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "While an agent works on subtree A, a human edits a node in subtree B: Confirm applies both cleanly, no dialog needed",
      "evaluator": "human"
    },
    {
      "statement": "A human edits the same node the agent is rewriting: the review panel flags exactly that node as conflicted, shows both versions, and the human's edit survives unless the agent's is explicitly chosen",
      "evaluator": "human"
    },
    {
      "statement": "Rebase/merge logic is pure and unit-tested: agent-only change applies; human-only change persists; both-changed same-field flags a conflict",
      "evaluator": "executable"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkktWXdRLjOCdE05-2bATPtp_WpORR-02PudsA.description:
{
  "outcome": "Everything that needs the human — a permission request, an agent question, a finished draft, a stall — is an attention item attributed to its session and held in a queue. The session you are looking at shows its items inline as today; a background session badges the picker and toasts compactly instead of interrupting. No two sessions ever fight over one global modal.",
  "handle": "attention-inbox",
  "spec": "Today permission requests and questions land in one global askConfirm/AskDialog; a background session's request would interrupt whatever the human is viewing, or collide with another session's dialog. The requests are already promise-shaped, so the change is parking resolvers in a per-session queue instead of opening a modal. Cheap once [[session-registry]] exists; needed before more than two sessions is pleasant.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Background session A requests permission while the human chats in session B: B is not interrupted, A shows 'needs you' in the picker, and answering it from the picker resumes A",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkk68gAILhnLS9XtgzK63yLun0e57OoYw4GPWX.description:
{
  "outcome": "Every session's propose_edits opens or updates that session's own draft. Multiple drafts coexist — each independently reviewable, editable, and confirmable in any order — and confirming one automatically rebases the others against the tree it just changed, flagging anything newly conflicted. A second agent can no longer clobber or merge into the first agent's pending draft.",
  "handle": "per-session-drafts",
  "spec": "Today DraftState is {diff, projectId, decisions} — one draft per window (redux/ephemeral/draft/reducer.ts); a second session's propose_edits clobbers or merges into the first. Key the slice by sessionId and carry the baseline from [[baseline-rebase]], whose rebase machinery drives confirm-time rebasing of the remaining drafts. Relies-on [[session-registry]] for session identity in the renderer. Map overlay: one draft at a time via the selector; a composite view is a later nicety.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Sessions A and B each propose edits; both drafts are visible, independently reviewable, and confirmable in either order with correct results",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkDz8vfLR19fQNosIw5k_evWqohQExw8WRKiV.description:
{
  "outcome": "A session works a declared territory: its scope (one or more subtree roots) travels with it in the registry and picker, filters what it reads, and renders on the map as a per-session presence tint — so a glance shows which agent is working where, and two sessions converging on the same region is visible before it becomes a conflict.",
  "handle": "session-scope",
  "spec": "Today every session sees and can propose against the whole tree, and nothing on the map indicates an agent is working a region. Scope is coordination, not enforcement: out-of-scope proposals warn in review rather than being blocked (revisit if agents wander destructively). Map presence reuses the pattern of multi-user realtime presence (redux/ephemeral/realtime-info). Relies-on [[session-registry]] (scope lives there) and the overlap warning from [[per-session-drafts]].",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Session A scoped to branch X and session B scoped to branch Y: the map shows both territories, each agent's reads and proposals stay in its lane by default, and overlap is visible before it becomes a conflict",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkk8Gqe3lW6DiOdgxjcUlGIgaODX0ebzKQLhCJ.description:
{
  "outcome": "Concurrency holds up across windows and across trees: sessions stream independently per window, shared chat history cannot be corrupted by two writers, a session outlives the window that started it and any window can pick it up (exactly one driving it at a time), and edits arriving over the network rebase the same as local ones.",
  "handle": "cross-window",
  "spec": "Each window is its own renderer with its own WebSocket to the sidecar, and sessions are id-multiplexed — that part already works; the sidecar also already keeps sessions alive when a window closes. The real risks are shared-state races (chatHistory.ts serializes the whole per-project store on write) and orphaned/doubly-driven sessions. Cross-tree needs no new machinery beyond the per-project scoping that exists; tree-level concurrency across windows rides on Holochain's conductor sync and is verified against [[baseline-rebase]]. Mostly verification plus the chatHistory write-race fix, which can land any time.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Two windows, two different trees, an agent running in each: both stream independently, histories don't corrupt, closing one window doesn't kill its session, and the other window can pick it up",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}
